# SkillBridge — SDD Workflow Rules

This file defines the Spec-Driven Development (SDD) workflow for the
project. It is loaded on every session where work involves a `feat`
change as defined in `CLAUDE.md` Rule 13.

This file states operationally what the agent must do. For absolute
behavior rules (no inference, read files, approval gates, English only,
etc.), see `CLAUDE.md`. For code conventions, see `conventions.md`. For
testing, see `testing.md`. For architectural decisions, see
`ARCHITECTURE.md`.

If any rule in this file appears to conflict with `CLAUDE.md`,
`CLAUDE.md` wins.

---

## When this workflow applies

This workflow is triggered by any `feat` change that adds or modifies
domain behavior (see Rule 13 in `CLAUDE.md`).

It is skipped for `fix`, `chore`, `refactor`, `docs`, `test`, and
`style` changes, unless they touch domain invariants — validators,
model `clean()`, manager logic, `on_delete` policies, `AUTH_USER_MODEL`,
or GDPR-related code paths.

When in doubt, treat the change as requiring a spec and ask.

---

## Spec location and naming

Spec artifacts live at the monorepo root in `specs/<NNN-slug>/`.

- Three-digit sequential numbering: `001`, `002`, … (expands beyond
  three digits if needed).
- Kebab-case semantic slug: short (3-5 words), in English, describing
  the feature by its domain meaning — not by file or task.

Each spec folder contains three files:

- `spec.md` — what and why (no implementation detail)
- `plan.md` — how, including technical decisions
- `tasks.md` — numbered, atomic, individually approvable steps

Specs sit at the monorepo root because they describe domain features
independently of the implementation. Commands triggered by a spec
(tests, migrations, code) still execute via Docker per `CLAUDE.md`
Rule 12.

Examples of valid slugs:
- `001-status-history`
- `002-job-posting`
- `003-proposal-submission`

Examples of invalid slugs (do not use):
- `001-add-status-history-model` (describes the task, not the feature)
- `001-statushistory` (no hyphens)
- `001-models-jobs` (describes files, not feature)

